Author: Armaan Sandhu <<author email>>
Date:   Sun Nov 1 18:31:23 2026 -0500

    sd-journal: don't compare all files for every entry
    
    to find the next entry, real_journal_next() looks at the candidate of
    every journal file. but between two entries only the file we just read
    from has a new candidate, and with rotated files that file nearly always
    wins again, so reading the journal costs O(files) per entry for nothing.
    
    remember the runner-up of a full scan. if the next candidate of the
    winner still comes before the runner-up, it comes before all the other
    files too. anything else falls back to the full scan, which also runs at
    least once per second, so the other files still get their tail timestamp
    refresh and new entries get noticed.
    
    a file at EOF has no candidate to compare, so a new entry in it could
    sort before the winner's next one and would be lost once the reader moved
    past it. hence, reading forward, don't reuse a scan if a file that isn't
    archived was at EOF, unless the journal was opened with
    SD_JOURNAL_ASSUME_IMMUTABLE. the state is read before the lookup, since
    the file could get a new entry and be archived right after. the first
    scan after a seek isn't reused either, as the second one retries files
    where the seek lookup found nothing.
    
    tracking the runner-up doubles the comparisons of a scan, so it's only
    done after a file has won twice in a row. without this, 100 interleaved
    files got 1.85x slower. next_beyond_location() and compare_locations()
    now have several callers, but must stay inlined in the scan loop for it
    not to get slower, hence force that.
    
    journalctl -o cat, 1M entries in rotated files, elapsed time:
    
      100 files:   0.7s -> 0.27s
      1000 files:  6.3s -> 0.30s
    
    on a real 3.1G journal with 100 files and 5.6M entries it goes from 6.2s
    to 3.5s, and 100 fully interleaved files are unchanged.

